# **************************************************************************
# * WHAT THIS SHOWS
# *
# * The difference between a MAGMA and a GROUP, as two animations. Both are
# * 3x3 operation tables; only one is a group, and you will be able to tell
# * at a glance.
# **************************************************************************
;
def u:frames(t, n) { reshape(transpose(one_hot(flatten(t), n)), [n, n, n]) }  # frame k = cells where a*b = k
def u:fight(x, y) { if eq(mod(x - y + 3, 3), 1) { x } else { y } }  # Rock-Paper-Scissors
def u:add3(x, y) { mod(x + y, 3) }  # Z3, addition mod 3
# **************************************************************************
# * HOW TO READ BOTH ANIMATIONS
# *
# * Each one is a single 3x3 Cayley table, cycling through 3 frames. Rows and
# * columns are the elements 0, 1, 2. In frame k a cell lights up when
# * row * column = k -- so frame 0 shows every pairing that produces 0,
# * frame 1 every pairing that produces 1, and so on.
# *
# * ANIMATION 1 is Rock-Paper-Scissors. The lit cells clump and drift: some
# * rows get two lit cells in a frame while other rows get none. Rock wins
# * three of the nine matchups, scissors only two. Nothing is regular.
# **************************************************************************
svg(u:frames(table(:u:fight, range(3), range(3)), 3), "life")
# **************************************************************************
# * ANIMATION 2 is Z3, addition modulo 3.
# *
# * Every frame lights exactly ONE cell per row and ONE per column. That
# * shape is a permutation matrix.
# **************************************************************************
svg(u:frames(table(:u:add3, range(3), range(3)), 3), "life")
# **************************************************************************
# * WHAT TO NOTICE
# *
# * A table whose frames are all permutation matrices is a LATIN SQUARE:
# * every element appears exactly once in each row and once in each column.
# *
# * THE POINT -- the Latin square IS what being a group looks like. Each row
# * is a shuffle of the elements, which is another way of saying nothing is
# * ever lost and every element has an inverse. You can recognize a group
# * across the room without reading a single number.
# *
# * Both animations come from sw-MLPL's built-in svg(frames, "life")
# * renderer. There is no hand-written SVG in this demo at all.
# **************************************************************************
